There is no cure for MCAS, but treatment can help control symptoms. Treatment is usually started **one step at a time**, so you and your doctor can see what helps.

## Antihistamines (H1 blockers)

**Non-drowsy antihistamines**, such as **cetirizine**, **loratadine** or **fexofenadine**, are usually tried first. They block histamine and can help with itching, flushing, hives and a runny nose.

A specialist may suggest a **higher dose** than usual. Only do this if your doctor or specialist tells you to. Read about [fexofenadine](/medicines/fexofenadine/) and [loratadine](/medicines/loratadine/).

## H2 blockers

**H2 blockers**, such as **famotidine**, block histamine in a different way. They can help with **gut symptoms** such as tummy pain, indigestion and feeling sick. They are often taken alongside an H1 antihistamine. Read about [famotidine](/medicines/famotidine/).

## Mast cell stabilisers

**Sodium cromoglicate** helps stop mast cells releasing their chemicals. For gut symptoms it is taken by mouth, usually before meals. It is normally started by a specialist.

## Other medicines

- **Montelukast** blocks other chemicals that mast cells release, called leukotrienes. It may help with wheezing and other symptoms.
- **Steroids** are sometimes used for a short time during a bad flare.
- Your specialist may suggest other medicines if these do not help.

## Adrenaline auto-injectors

If you have had **anaphylaxis**, or are at risk of it, you should be given **2 adrenaline auto-injectors** to carry with you at all times. Make sure you, your family and people you live or work with know **how and when to use them**.

{% callout type="warn" title="Some medicines can trigger symptoms" %}
Some medicines, such as **anti-inflammatory painkillers** (for example ibuprofen), some **opioid painkillers** and some **anaesthetics**, can trigger mast cells in some people. Tell every doctor, dentist and pharmacist that you have MCAS before starting a new medicine or having a procedure.
{% /callout %}

## Avoiding triggers

Medicines work best alongside **avoiding your triggers**. Read [living with MCAS](/conditions/respiratory-and-allergy/mast-cell-activation-syndrome/living-well/) for practical tips.
